    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Adolescent stress is a common occurrence in most mammals’ lives, especially during weaning and the transition to independence. Early stress has often been linked to the development of psychiatric disorders later in life (Toledo-Rodriguez and Sandi, 2011; Ito et al., 2006; Lupien et al., 2009), but more recent studies have found that early stressful experiences that are not overwhelming may calibrate development and lead to increased resilience and arousal regulation in adulthood (Lyons et al., 2009; Kendig et al., 2011). The idea that mild early stress may be involved in developmental calibration is called stress inoculation, and it has been shown to have effects on both behavior and physiological changes in the brain. I use a mouse model to investigate whether early social stress in mice increases resilience and stress-coping behaviors later in life. Juvenile mice were exposed to eleven 10 minute sessions of social stress, and were subsequently tested for changes in stress-coping, novelty-seeking, reward-seeking, and risk-taking behaviors. Based on the results of the Tail Suspension, Open Field, Novel Object Exploration, and Sucrose Preference Tests, stress inoculation leads to an increase in indications of stress-coping and some novelty-seeking and risk-taking behaviors, but not reward-seeking.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
